## Ariadne contract review: S20-770 required contract index (rev 1) @ 9dc78fe

**status: FAIL** (three P1s; no P0). The design is right and the gate is real. Two of the twelve rows point at the wrong defining artifact, and the registry change this work made is not recorded by the ADR that the registry cites for it.

### Findings

**P1-1 — 17.1 mis-attributes the entity body kinds.** `MUTATION_SCHEMA_V1.md` is S20-340, a schema *generation* spec. It parses "the eighteen `entity` declarations and their named body `record` declarations" out of `docs/spec/SSMC1_EPOCH1_SCHEMA.txt` (BLAKE3 pinned `1983bc8d…`), and says of itself that it "describes what later candidate construction may express" and cannot construct a mutation. The phrase "entity body kinds" does not occur in it. The definer is the epoch-1 manifest; MUTATION_SCHEMA_V1.md is the generated descriptor layer over it. The SCB1 §2 and OBJECT_STORE_V1.md citations in that row are exact (SCB1:50 carries `sley2.object.v1`; OBJECT_STORE_V1.md has "Stored object record").

**P1-2 — 17.12 cites sections that do not define its listed domains.** `sley2.protocol-frame.v1 -> ProtocolFrameId` is defined at `SMP1.md:41`, inside **section 1, Framing** — not sections 2 or 3. Section 2 is the handshake (`SMP1.md:95` gives the preimage). Section 3, "Sessions and request identity", explicitly defers session identity to S20-330 and freezes only scoping rules; the `sley2.session.v1` preimage lives in Appendix A (`SMP1.md:345`). So the row cites a section defining none of its listed domains and omits the section defining one that is listed. Either "sections 1 and 2", or "sections 1, 2, and 3" with `sley2.session.v1` added.

**P1-3 — ADR-0047 does not record the registry change attributed to it.** `IDENTIFIERS_V1.md:72` reads "Four rows were added on 2026-09-03 (ADR-0047)", and that same document rules that "adding a domain requires an ADR, fixtures, and registry drift validation". ADR-0047 has no decision item on the four domains, never names them, and records no fixtures. Meanwhile the index Boundary and the ADR status both say the work "changes no identity", while `machine-summary.json` records `domains_added_to_registry` with those four entries. ADR-0048 does this correctly and is the model. Fix by adding the decision item plus fixtures reference, and restating the boundary as deriving no new identifier while registering four already-derived domains.

**P2-4 — registry completeness is half-checked.** The drift loop (checker lines 94-104) flags derived-but-unregistered only; a registry row no crate derives never fails. ADR-0048 decision 5 claims the claim and the check "cannot drift apart again", and the registry asserts a count, but neither the reverse direction nor the count is checked. I compared both directions at this commit: 50 registry rows, 50 domains derived under `crates/`, zero difference either way. So the registry does match the implementation exactly today; the gap is durability, not accuracy.

**P2-5 — the count prose has already drifted.** `IDENTIFIERS_V1.md:72` says "sixteen more later the same day (ADR-0048)". ADR-0048 says fifteen, commit `0d57ec5` added exactly fifteen rows, and 31 + 4 + 15 = 50. Read "fifteen".

**P2-6 — section 5 under-describes its own checker.** It lists four behaviours; the checker also runs the identifier-registry drift over every crate, validates machine-summary status and fields, checks the ERROR_CODES symbols, and requires the WORK_PACKAGES reference. The drift behaviour is the one an S20-110 reader most needs to find here.

**P2-7 — 77000/77001 are reserved but never emitted.** The checker verifies both symbols exist in `ERROR_CODES_V1.md`, then classifies nothing: every failure is an untyped `problems` string, so `identifier-registry-drift:*` maps to neither code.

**P2-8 — defining artifacts are checked only as `.md` under `docs/spec`** (lines 66-71); a non-`.md` definer is silently skipped. This is what blocks P1-1 as written: `SSMC1_EPOCH1_SCHEMA.txt` cannot currently be named and checked.

**P2-9 — section citations are unverified prose.** File existence is checked; "section 2", "sections 2 and 3", "section on the execution envelope" are not. That is how P1-2 reached review.

**P3-10 —** 17.9 and 17.10 say "section on the execution envelope" / "section on the test envelope" where the document is numbered (§2 and §5; §6 and §7). **P3-11 —** in `REPORT_ENVELOPE_PROFILE_V1.md`, `### 9.1` sits at line 283, after `## 10` at line 261; 17.10 depends on that pointer.

### Answers to the four posed questions

- **Do the four new domains belong in IDENTIFIERS_V1.md?** Yes, and the call is correct. Domain separation is a registry-level property: a reviewer confirms no two preimages share a domain by reading one table. A domain living only in its owning contract cannot be checked for collision. ADR-0048 states this reasoning; ADR-0047 should have.
- **Does the registry now match the implementation exactly?** Yes at 9dc78fe, verified both directions. But only one direction is enforced (P2-4), and the prose count is already wrong (P2-5).
- **Is index-not-restate the right relationship?** Yes, with the "document wins" tiebreak. Restating frozen rules creates a second source that drifts; the index accepts pointer error as the cheaper failure mode. P1-1 and P1-2 are exactly that failure mode, so the mitigation is checking the pointers (P2-9), not restating.
- **Do all twelve rows name the actual definer?** Ten do. 17.1 and 17.12 do not. Separately, the twelve required names appear nowhere in the repository except this index and ADR-0047, and the master goal is not in-tree or cited by path, so fidelity of the twelve names to section 17 is unverifiable from the worktree and unchecked by the gate. Noting it as a scope boundary rather than a finding, since the composed authority is external by construction.

### validation

Read-only inspection at pinned worktree 9dc78fe; no `make` target, no cargo. Ran the staged checker directly (`python3 scripts/check_required_contract_index.py`): `result: PASS`, `problems: []`, exit 0, status `S20_770_CONTRACT_DRAFT_REVIEW_PENDING`, 12 rows / 19 documents / 18 checkers / 14 domains / 50 derived domains. `git status --porcelain` empty after. Confirmed all 18 named checkers exist and are invoked by the Makefile, the index checker runs under `quick` (Makefile:72), and all 19 named documents exist. Confirmed 17.10's "checkable reason" is genuine: `check_report_envelope_profile.py:92-100` fails if `crates/sley-vm/src/execute.rs` gains any of the four TestCase resource-unit markers. V1 not run and not required for a read-only document review.

### blockers

None. No repository mutation, no spend, no deploy.

### return_to

Orchestrator. P1-1 and P1-2 are bounded Sley documentation edits; P1-3 is an ADR amendment. P2-4 and P2-8 are checker changes and touch the S20-110 registry gate, so the placement of registry-drift enforcement inside an S20-770 checker is worth a line from Nabu before Merlin executes.

